  "title": "Types of Sets",
  "body": " Types of Sets       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.1 Sets    Specific Learning Outcomes  Identify set notations and elements of a set in different situations          Sorting Set Cards   Six cards show these sets: , , , , and .     Which of the first three cards has no elements, which has a number of elements you can count to the end, and which goes on for ever?    Compare with . What is the same, and what is different?    Compare the two letter cards.        Types of Sets    The empty set (or null set) has no elements. It is written or , and . Note that is not empty: it has one element, .  A finite set has a number of elements that can be counted to the end. An infinite set goes on for ever.  The universal set , (read xi ), is the set of all the elements being considered in a problem. Some books write or .  Equal sets have exactly the same elements: . Equivalent sets have the same number of elements: . Equal sets are always equivalent.  means is a subset of : every element of is also in . Every set is a subset of itself, and is a subset of every set.    The subsets of are , , and : . The subsets of are , , , , , , and : . Each element is either in a subset or not, so a set with elements has subsets.   The symbols , and join the list in .   Empty, Finite or Infinite   Is each set empty, finite (but not empty) or infinite?           (a) only for , which is not a natural number: the set is . (b) is a whole number, so the set is : finite, not empty. (c) goes on for ever: infinite.     Equal or Equivalent   , and . Compare with , and with .    and : both have elements but the elements differ, so and are equivalent but not equal. has exactly the elements of in a different order, so .     Listing Subsets   List the subsets of . Is ? Is ?    The subsets are , , and : four subsets. because and are both in the larger set. is not a subset, because .    Type a set inside curly brackets with commas between the elements, for example {2, 5, 9} . On most phone keyboards the curly brackets are on the second page of symbols: tap ?123 (or 123 ), then =\\< (or #+= ). Type the empty set as {} . Type a count as a whole number.             Is each set empty, finite (but not empty) or infinite?          (a) Empty. (b) Finite ( elements). (c) Infinite.    and . Are and equal, equivalent or neither?  Equivalent, not equal: has elements, like .    List all the subsets of . How many subsets does have?  , , , ; subsets    , is the set of multiples of in , and is the set of even numbers in . List . Is ?  . No: .    True or false? (a) (b) (c)  (a) True. (b) True. (c) False.    Challenge: Counting Subsets  How many subsets does a set with elements have? A set has subsets: how many elements does it have?  ; , because .    "
