  "title": "Evaluating Numerical Cases Using Binomial Expansion",
  "body": " Evaluating Numerical Cases Using Binomial Expansion       Strand  1.0 Numbers and Algebra    Sub-Strand  1.2 Binomial Expansions    Specific Learning Outcomes  Evaluate binomial expressions up to the power of 10 in different situations           Approximating Powers of Numbers   Close to One      Expand .    Put and work out each term as a decimal.    Add the first two, the first three and the first four terms. Compare each sum with a calculator value of .    Repeat for , using in .    How many terms do you need to give each value correct to 4 decimal places?        Using an Expansion to Find a Power  To estimate a power of a number close to 1 (or close to a whole number ):  write the number as (or ) with small, keeping its sign;  expand (or ) using Pascal's triangle;  work out each term exactly as a decimal, and keep adding terms until the next term is smaller than ;  round the sum to 4 decimal places.    When is small, each term is much smaller than the one before, so a few terms give the value to the accuracy you need.    Spreadsheet Check  In a spreadsheet, put in column A, the numbers from the row of Pascal's triangle in column B, and each term in column C (for with , type =B1*0.02^A1 and fill down). In column D keep a running total, for example =SUM(C$1:C1) . Watch how the total settles after a few rows.    Drag and to compare (solid) with its 2-term (dashed), 3-term (dotted) and 4-term (long dashes) approximations. For large and far from 0 the curves leave the graph; the table still gives the values.     Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   , so put into : The next term is , smaller than , so stop. The sum is , so . A calculator gives , which also rounds to .     Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   , so put into : The fifth term, , is not smaller than , so it is included; the next term is , so stop there. The sum is , so . A calculator gives , also . The terms alternate in sign because is negative.     Estimating  Use the expansion of to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   Put . Each term has a falling power of 2: The sum is , so . A calculator gives .    Type decimals with a point, for example 1.1041 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for.                Estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   ; the next term is . So .    Estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.   With : ; the next term is . So .    Use the expansion of to find , correct to 4 decimal places. Why is the full sum of the terms exact?  ; has only five terms  , so . The expansion of a fourth power has only five terms, so adding all of them gives the exact value.    Expand up to the term in , and use your answer to estimate , correct to 4 decimal places.  ;  . Since , put : , so .    What value of would you use to estimate (a) from (b) from ?  (a) (b)    Estimate using , correct to 2 decimal places.   , so .    Challenge: When Is Not Small  Use the first four terms of to estimate , and compare with a calculator. Why is this estimate poorer than the one for ?  Work out the fifth term.  ; the calculator gives . With the fifth term is , far from negligible.  . The fifth term is , so the later terms still matter: is not small. For the fifth term is only .       Binomial Expansions at Work   Growing Savings in a SACCO   A youth group in Nyeri saves Ksh in a SACCO account that adds interest at the end of each year.   How much is in the account after one year?    Write the amount after 5 years as a power.    Use the first three terms of with to estimate the amount.    The SACCO statement shows Ksh . By how much is your estimate short?    Which term would you add next, and what is it worth?        Growth and Decay by Expansion  An amount that grows by in each period is after periods. A value that falls by in each period is . Put (or for a loss) and expand:   The first term is the starting amount. The second, , is the simple interest (or the simple loss) over periods. The later terms are the interest earned on interest.    Where Binomial Expansions Are Used  Back to the question at the start of the chapter: where do we use binomial expansions? In savings and loans, as in this lesson; in population growth and in the depreciation of machines and vehicles; in measurement, where a tile or a plank cut slightly too big has an area or volume that an expansion estimates quickly; in quick estimates in engineering and science; and, later in mathematics, in probability. Discuss with your partner: where else does a quantity grow or shrink by the same percentage again and again?    Interest in a SACCO  Ksh is saved in a SACCO in Nyeri at a year, added at the end of each year. Estimate the amount after 4 years using (a) three terms (b) four terms of the expansion.   The amount is . With , .  (a) = Ksh .  (b) Adding : = Ksh . The SACCO statement shows Ksh .     A Boda Boda Loses Value  A boda boda bought in Kisumu for Ksh loses of its value each year. Use four terms of an expansion to estimate its value after 6 years, to the nearest hundred shillings.   The value is . With , the first four terms of are so the value is about = Ksh . (The exact value is Ksh , which is also Ksh to the nearest hundred shillings.)     A Loan on a Phone App  Wanjiku borrows Ksh through a phone app that charges interest a day, compounded daily. She repays after 10 days. Estimate the amount she owes, using four terms.   She owes . With , the row for power 10 starts 1, 10, 45, 120: so she owes about = Ksh (a calculator also gives Ksh ). The interest is about Ksh in only 10 days: more than of the loan, because of the later terms.  Before borrowing on a phone, read the daily rate and work out the real cost. At a day, a loan kept for a month costs about of the amount borrowed.    Type decimals with a point, for example 1.1041 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for.            A town in Nakuru County has people, and its population grows by a year. Estimate the population after 5 years using (a) three terms (b) four terms of an expansion.  (a) (b) , about people  (a) . (b) Adding gives , about people (the exact value is ).    A posho mill bought for Ksh loses of its value each year. Use the expansion of to find its value after 3 years. Why is your answer exact?  Ksh ; has only four terms  , so the value is = Ksh . The expansion of a cube has only four terms, and all four were used.    Ksh is placed in a fixed deposit at a month, compounded monthly, for 8 months. Use four terms to estimate the amount to the nearest shilling.  Ksh  , so the amount is about = Ksh , that is Ksh (exact Ksh ).     A phone app lends Ksh at a day, compounded daily, for 6 days.  Estimate the amount owed, using four terms.  A friend says the interest is , so Ksh . Explain why the app charges more.    (a) About Ksh (b) the third and fourth terms are interest on interest  (a) , so the amount is about = Ksh , interest about Ksh . (b) is only the second term (the simple interest); the third and fourth terms add interest charged on interest already owed.    The first two terms of an estimate for a savings account after 8 years are . What is the yearly rate of interest?   The second term is , so and : the rate is a year.    A square floor tile should be wide but is cut cm wide. Use to find its area and the extra area.  ; extra  . The tile should be , so the extra area is .    Challenge: Powers of 11 and 101  Use to find , and explain why its digits are not 1 5 10 10 5 1. Then find using .  Think about carrying.  ;  . The coefficients 10 are two-digit numbers, so they carry into the next place. With each coefficient has two places to itself: , the row 1, 4, 6, 4, 1 with zeros between.     "
