  "title": "Shear",
  "body": " Shear       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.1 Matrices and Transformations    Specific Learning Outcomes  Perform transformation involving shear and stretch in different situations          Pushing a Pile of Books   A pile of exercise books is 5 cm high. Its side is pushed with a slanting ruler so that the top book moves 2 cm sideways and the bottom book stays on the desk.     Work out (distance moved) (height above the desk) for the top book. The book halfway up, 2.5 cm above the desk, moved 1 cm: work out the same ratio for it.    Which book did not move?    On graph paper, draw the rectangle with corners , , and . Move every point parallel to the -axis by times its height above the -axis. Find and draw the image.    Find the images of and and write down the matrix.    Find the areas of the rectangle and of its image.        Shear  A shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor moves every point parallel to the -axis by times its distance from the -axis. Points on the -axis do not move: it is the invariant axis . When , points above the -axis move in the positive -direction and points below it move the opposite way. A shear with the -axis invariant moves every point parallel to the -axis; when , points to the right of the -axis move in the positive -direction.  The shear factor is counted negative when points above (or to the right of) the axis move in the negative direction. Every point slides parallel to the invariant axis, by the shear factor times its distance from that axis, so areas do not change. Using the columns rule: Both have determinant .  To describe a shear fully, give its invariant axis and its shear factor. Its inverse is the shear with the same invariant axis and shear factor .    A shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor . The image of the rectangle is dashed.   The rectangle O(0, 0), (3, 0), (3, 2), (0, 2), drawn solid, and its dashed image (0, 0), (3, 0), (6, 2), (3, 2), a parallelogram. The base on the thick x-axis stays. Arrows of length 3 show the top corners sliding right; an arrow of length 1.5 shows a point at height 1 sliding half as far.      Move the slider. The top edge slides, the base on the invariant axis stays, and the area stays the same.     Shearing a Rectangle   The rectangle with corners , , and is mapped by a shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor . Find the matrix, the image and the areas.    With the -axis invariant, stays at and moves up to . The matrix is .   The image is , , , . Points 2 units from the -axis moved up ; points 4 units away moved up .  The rectangle has area . The image is a parallelogram with vertical sides of length , 2 units apart: area again.     Describing a Shear   A shear maps the square , , , onto , , , . Describe the shear fully and write down its matrix.    Every -coordinate is unchanged, so the points move parallel to the -axis, and the -axis is the invariant axis.  moved to : a distance at distance from the axis, so . Check with : it moved at distance , and .  The transformation is a shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor . Its matrix is .     A Leaning Bookshelf   A bookshelf in a school library is 1.8 m tall and 0.9 m wide. Its joints work loose, and the top moves 0.3 m sideways while the bottom stays on the floor. Treat the front of the shelf as a rectangle sheared with the floor as the invariant axis. Find the shear factor, how far a point 0.9 m above the floor moves, and the area of the front of the shelf after the shear.    Shear factor = distance moved distance from the invariant axis .  A point 0.9 m above the floor moves , half as far as the top.  A shear does not change area, so the front still has area ( ).     The front of a bookshelf before and after its top moves 0.3 m sideways.   A rectangle 0.9 m wide and 1.8 m tall standing on the floor, drawn solid, and its dashed sheared image whose top edge has moved 0.3 m to the right. The floor is drawn thick. The shear factor is 0.3 divided by 1.8.     Type one number in each box of a matrix. Type fractions with a slash, for example -3\/2 , or as exact decimals, for example -1.5 . Type each coordinate in its own box.             Find the image of the point under each shear.  The -axis invariant, shear factor ; the point .  The -axis invariant, shear factor ; the point .    (a) (b)    A shear maps , , , onto , , , . Describe the shear fully and write down its matrix.  A shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor ;  The -coordinates do not change and the points on the -axis stay, so the -axis is invariant. moved at distance : shear factor .    Describe fully the transformation with matrix , and find the image of .  A shear with the -axis invariant and shear factor ;    A rectangular plot 25 m by 12 m is drawn on a plan and then sheared. What is the area of the image? Give a reason.  , because a shear has determinant and so does not change area.    Show that every point on the -axis is fixed by . Find the images of and , and explain why both moved the same distance.  ; and : both moved 5 units, because both are 2 units from the invariant axis.  . and . Each moves units: the distance moved depends only on the distance from the invariant axis.    Challenge: A Shear With Another Invariant Line (Extension)  is the matrix of a shear whose invariant line is not an axis.  Find .  Show that is fixed by , and that every point on the line is fixed.  Find the image of and show that it moved parallel to the line .    A fixed point satisfies .  (a) (b) (c) , which moved by  (a) , so areas are unchanged.  (b) . A point on is , and .  (c) . The point moved by , which is , a vector along the line .    "
