  "title": "Angle Bisector Loci",
  "body": " Angle Bisector Loci       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.3 Loci    Specific Learning Outcomes  Construct loci of perpendicular line and angle bisectors          The Corner of the Classroom   The two walls of a classroom meet the floor in two straight edges that make a right angle at the corner .     Construct the bisector of the corner angle with compasses. Measure the angle it makes with each wall.    Choose a point on your bisector. Use a set square to draw the perpendicular from to each wall, and measure both distances.    Choose a point that is not on the bisector and measure its two distances in the same way.    On a new sheet, draw two straight lines that cross at . Find several points that are equidistant from both lines, in all four angles. What do they lie on? Measure the angle between the lines you find.        The Angle Bisector Locus  The locus of points equidistant from the two arms of an angle is the angle bisector , the line through the vertex that cuts the angle into two equal halves. Every point on the bisector is the same distance from both arms, and every point inside the angle that is the same distance from both arms is on the bisector.  Why: if is on the bisector of angle and , are the feet of the perpendiculars from to and , the triangles and have a right angle, the common hypotenuse and equal angles at , so they are congruent and .  The locus of points equidistant from two straight lines that cross is the pair of bisectors of the angles between them. The two bisectors are at right angles to each other.  To construct the bisector of an angle: with the compass point at the vertex, draw an arc cutting both arms; from the two points where it cuts, draw equal arcs that cross inside the angle; join the vertex to the crossing point. Keep the arcs. The bisectors of the three angles of a triangle meet at the incentre , which is equidistant from the three sides.    Bisecting angle of : every point on the bisector has .   An angle XOY of 64 degrees with vertex O. An arc centred at O cuts OX at A and OY at B. Equal arcs from A and from B cross at C. The bisector OC is drawn. P is on the bisector, with perpendiculars PF to OX and PG to OY, right-angle marks at F and G, and PF and PG marked equal.      Drag until its distances to the two arms are equal: it leaves a dot. Use Next and Back to step through the construction. Tick the boxes to show the bisector and to see what happens with two whole lines.     Bisecting an Angle of   Draw an angle of and construct its bisector.     Step 1: With the compass point at , draw an arc that cuts at and at .   Step 2: With the compasses open to a suitable radius, the same for both, draw an arc from and an arc from inside the angle so that they cross at .   Step 3: Join to and extend the line. This is the bisector ( ).  Check with a protractor: each half should measure . It works because and , so triangles and are congruent and the angles at are equal.     Equal Distances to the Arms   is on the bisector of angle . on and on are the feet of the perpendiculars from . and . Find , and , and angle to the nearest degree.     is on the bisector, so it is the same distance from both arms: .  In the right-angled triangle : , so . In the same way .   , so and , about .     Two Roads that Cross   Two straight roads cross at an angle of . A water point is to be the same distance from both roads. Describe where it can be.    The roads are two whole lines, so the water point can be on either bisector of the angles between them ( ). One bisector halves the angles and makes with the first road. The other halves the angles and makes with the first road, measured the same way round. The two bisectors meet at .   Two roads crossing at and the two bisectors of the angles between them.   Two solid lines, the roads, cross at O at 50 degrees. Two dashed lines through O are the angle bisectors: one at 25 degrees to the first road, inside the 50 degree angle, and one at 115 degrees, inside the 130 degree angle. A right-angle mark shows that the bisectors are perpendicular.       Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign, for example 74 . Type coordinates as whole numbers, for example -3 .            Draw an angle of and construct its bisector. What should each half measure?     is on the bisector of angle . on and on are the feet of the perpendiculars from , with and . Find and .  ,  because is on the bisector. , so .    Two straight lines cross at . Give the angles that the two bisectors of the angles between them make with the first line, and the angle between the two bisectors.  and ;    A triangular lawn at a school in Nyeri has sides , and . A sprinkler is to be placed the same distance from all three edges. Using to represent , construct its position. How far is it from each edge?  At the incentre, from each edge.  Construct the triangle and the bisectors of two of its angles; they meet at the incentre . The triangle is right-angled ( ), so the distance is , which you can check by measuring the perpendicular from to each side: on the drawing.    In triangle , angle and angle . The bisectors of angles and meet at . Find angle .   Angle . In triangle , angle and angle , so angle .    Challenge: Why the Bisectors Meet at Right Angles  Two straight lines cross, making angles of and . Show that the bisectors of these two angles are perpendicular.  The two angles are next to each other on a straight line. Add their halves.  The angle between the bisectors is degrees.  The bisectors lie inside two adjacent angles, so the angle between them is half of one plus half of the other: . So the bisectors are perpendicular, whatever is.    "
