  "type": "Key Takeaway",
  "number": "7.4.2",
  "title": "The Angle Bisector Locus.",
  "body": " The Angle Bisector Locus  The locus of points equidistant from the two arms of an angle is the angle bisector , the line through the vertex that cuts the angle into two equal halves. Every point on the bisector is the same distance from both arms, and every point inside the angle that is the same distance from both arms is on the bisector.  Why: if is on the bisector of angle and , are the feet of the perpendiculars from to and , the triangles and have a right angle, the common hypotenuse and equal angles at , so they are congruent and .  The locus of points equidistant from two straight lines that cross is the pair of bisectors of the angles between them. The two bisectors are at right angles to each other.  To construct the bisector of an angle: with the compass point at the vertex, draw an arc cutting both arms; from the two points where it cuts, draw equal arcs that cross inside the angle; join the vertex to the crossing point. Keep the arcs. The bisectors of the three angles of a triangle meet at the incentre , which is equidistant from the three sides.  "
