  "title": "Constant Angle Loci",
  "body": " Constant Angle Loci       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.3 Loci    Specific Learning Outcomes  Construct loci of points given constant angle in different situations          A Card Angle Between Two Pins   Two pins and are apart. A card has a corner of . Slide the card so that one edge of the corner always touches and the other edge always touches .     Mark the position of the corner of the card in six different places.    Your marks lie on an arc. Find the centre of the circle it belongs to by constructing the perpendicular bisectors of two chords joining your marks.    Measure angle . How does it compare with the angle of the card?    Measure the radius .    Turn the card over and slide it on the other side of . What do you find?    Repeat with the card. What kind of arc do you get, and where is its centre?        Constant Angle Loci  Let be an angle between and . The locus of a point on one side of a segment such that is an arc of a circle through and . We say that  subtends the angle at every point of the arc: the segment fills the same angle at every point of the arc.    The centre is on the perpendicular bisector of .  For , is on the same side as and is on the major arc, with , and radius .  For the arc is a semicircle on as diameter, with centre the midpoint of .  For , is on the other side of and is on the minor arc; the radius is still .  Points on the other arc of the same circle see at , because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add to .  If may be on either side of , the locus is two arcs, mirror images of each other in (not including and ).    To construct the arc for : construct the perpendicular bisector of ; at , on 's side, draw a line making an angle of with ; it meets the bisector at ; draw the arc with centre and radius . For , draw the line at on the other side of .    Constructing the locus of with on one side of .   Segment AB of 7 cm with its perpendicular bisector through the midpoint M. At A a line makes 30 degrees with AB and meets the bisector at the centre O, 2.0 cm above M. A solid arc with centre O and radius 4.0 cm runs from A over the top to B; P on it has angle APB of 60 degrees. The mirror arc below AB is dashed.      Drag along the arc: the angle stays the same. Move the slider. Tick the boxes to show the centre and the arc on the other side. Drag inside and outside the arc.     The Locus for   . Construct the locus of a point on one side of such that . Find the distance of the centre from and the radius of the arc.     Step 1: Construct the perpendicular bisector of , meeting it at .   Step 2: At , on 's side, draw a line making with . It meets the bisector at the centre .   Step 3: Draw the arc with centre through and , on 's side ( ).  In the right-angled triangle , and : The centre is from and the radius is . Check: .     A Right Angle at   . Describe the locus of a point on one side of such that .    The angle in a semicircle is , so lies on the semicircle with diameter : centre the midpoint of , radius , not including and (panel (a) of ). The construction rule agrees: the line at makes with , so it meets the bisector at itself.     An Obtuse Angle   . Construct the locus of a point on one side of such that . What angle does subtend at points on the rest of the same circle?    The angle is more than , so the centre is on the other side of . At , on the side away from , draw a line making with . It meets the perpendicular bisector at , below . The radius is about . lies on the minor arc, on the far side of from (panel (b) of ).  A point on the major arc forms a cyclic quadrilateral , so .     (a) The semicircle on where . (b) The minor arc where ; on the major arc of the same circle the angle is .   Two panels. (a) AB of 9 cm with a semicircle on it, centre M the midpoint of AB, and a point P on the semicircle with a right angle marked at P. (b) AB of 6 cm; the centre O is below AB, 1.7 cm from its midpoint; a solid minor arc above AB has P on it with angle APB of 120 degrees; the rest of the circle is dashed below AB, with Q on it and angle AQB of 60 degrees.     Type decimals with a point, for example 12.6 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign, for example 74 .                . Construct the locus of a point on one side of such that . Give angle , angle and the radius of the arc.  , , radius  and . The radius is , about .    . Construct the locus of a point , on either side of , such that .  The circle with diameter : centre the midpoint of , radius , without the points and .    The goalposts of a school football pitch are apart. Using to represent , draw the locus of points on the pitch from which the goal subtends an angle of . What is the radius of the arc on the ground?  ( on the drawing)  At one post draw a line at to the goal line, on the pitch side; it meets the perpendicular bisector of the goal at the centre. The radius is , so the centre is as far from each post as the posts are from each other: triangle is equilateral.    The arc of a constant angle locus through and has radius , and . Find the angle subtends at points on the major arc, and at points on the minor arc.  on the major arc, on the minor arc  From , , so . Points on the minor arc see .    Points on the major arc of a circle with centre see at . What angle do points on the minor arc see, and what is angle ?  ;    Challenge: The Radius Formula  Show that the radius of the constant angle arc for an angle is .  Use triangle , where is the midpoint of , and find angle .   and bisects it (triangle is isosceles), so . In the right-angled triangle , with , so .    "
