  "number": "7.8",
  "title": "Loci Involving Chords",
  "body": " Loci Involving Chords       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.3 Loci    Specific Learning Outcomes  [Learning Outcome] (TODO: not yet provided in the curriculum design)          Equal Chords      Draw a circle with centre and radius . Draw five different chords, each long.    Mark the midpoint of each chord.    Measure the distance of each midpoint from .    Explain why. Use the fact from Grade 11 that the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects it.    Repeat for chords of . How far from are their midpoints?    What is the locus of the midpoints of all chords of length ?        Chords and Loci    The perpendicular bisector of any chord of a circle passes through the centre.  In a circle with centre and radius , the midpoints of all chords of length lie on the circle with centre and radius Equal chords are the same distance from the centre.  A chord as long as the diameter ( ) has its midpoint at , so the locus is the single point . No chord is longer than the diameter.      Drag the end of the chord round the circle. Its midpoint traces the locus. Change the chord length with the slider.     Midpoints of Equal Chords   A circle has centre and radius . Describe the locus of the midpoints of all chords of length .   Four chords of in a circle of radius . Their midpoints lie on the dashed circle of radius with the same centre.   A circle with centre O and radius 13 cm, with four chords of 24 cm in different directions. The midpoint of each chord is marked; all four lie on a dashed circle of radius 5 cm with centre O. One radius OA and the segment OM are drawn.       Let be the midpoint of a chord of . Then is perpendicular to and , so This is the same for every such chord. The locus is the circle with centre and radius .     Finding the Centre of a Broken Plate   Only part of the rim of a round plate is left. (a) Explain how to construct its centre. (b) On a grid, three points on the rim are , and . Find the centre and the radius.    (a) Mark three points , , on the rim and draw the chords and . Construct the perpendicular bisector of each. Both pass through the centre, so the centre is where they meet.  (b) and have the same -coordinate, so the perpendicular bisector of is the vertical line through its midpoint: . The centre is for some , and it is the same distance from and :  so and . The centre is and the radius is units.    Type decimals with a point, for example 12.6 . Give answers to the accuracy asked for. Choose from each list.        Chords of are drawn in a circle of radius . Describe the locus of their midpoints.  The circle with the same centre and radius .  , so for every such chord.    In a circle of radius , the midpoint of a chord is from the centre. How long is the chord?   Half the chord is , so the chord is .    Only part of the round concrete base of an old water tank in Kakamega is left. Explain how to find its centre with a tape measure, pegs and string.  Peg out two chords on the rim; find the perpendicular bisector of each; the centre is where they cross.  Put pegs , and on the rim. For chord , find the points equidistant from and with two equal lengths of string, one from each peg, on both sides of ; the line through them is the perpendicular bisector. Do the same for . Both lines pass through the centre, so it is where they cross.    Challenge: Chords Through a Point  is a fixed point inside a circle with centre . Find the locus of the midpoints of all the chords that pass through .  If is the midpoint of such a chord, what is angle ?  The circle with diameter .  The line from to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord, and the chord passes through , so angle . So subtends a right angle at : a constant angle locus, the circle with diameter (it includes , the midpoint of the diameter through , and , the midpoint of the chord perpendicular to ).    "
