  "body": " Angle Between Two Lines       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.4 Three Dimensional Geometry    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine the angle between two lines in a 3-D object          A Pyramid from Manila Paper   The net below makes a pyramid . Its base has a right angle at , with , and . The faces and also have right angles at , and . When the net is folded, the edge stands vertically above the corner , like the corner of a box sliced off.   The net of the pyramid (left) and the folded pyramid (right). Not drawn to scale.   Left: the net of pyramid VABC. The base ABC has a right angle at A, AB 9 cm, AC 12 cm and BC 15 cm. Face VAB is folded out below AB and face VAC to the left of AC, each with a right angle at A and VA 8 cm. Face VBC is folded out beyond BC, with VB the square root of 145 and VC the square root of 208. Right: the folded pyramid with VA vertical above A and right angles at A.        Draw the net on manila paper, cut it out and fold it into the pyramid. Glue the edges with tabs.    Measure angle , the angle between the edges and . Then calculate it in triangle .    Measure angle , the angle between the edges and at the top.    Calculate and by Pythagoras. Then use the cosine rule in triangle , with , to calculate angle .    Write your measured and calculated angles side by side. Why might they differ?        Angle Between Two Lines That Meet  Two lines that meet at a point lie in one triangle: the point where they meet and the other end of each line. To find the angle between them, find the triangle that contains both lines, then flatten it : sketch the triangle on its own and mark the sides you know.    Look for a right angle. An edge that is at right angles to a face is at right angles to every line in that face. In a cuboid, is at right angles to the face , so angle . Then use sine, cosine or tangent.  If the triangle has no right angle, find its three sides by Pythagoras and use the cosine rule from Grade 11. For the angle opposite the side :    An angle named by three letters, such as angle , has its vertex at the middle letter, . Such an angle may be obtuse. Two lines make two angles that add up to ; the angle between two lines means the one that is not obtuse, from to .    Choose two lines with the pair slider. The triangle that contains both is outlined and the angle between them is shown. Change the height and watch the angle change.     An Angle in a Right-Angled Triangle   The cuboid has , and . Find the angle between the space diagonal and the edge .    Step 1: the triangle. The lines meet at ; their other ends are and . So the angle is angle in triangle .  Step 2: a right angle?  is at right angles to the face , and lies in that face, so angle .  Step 3: the sides.  , and , so .  Step 4: the angle.  is next to angle and is the hypotenuse: so angle .     An Angle by the Cosine Rule   In the same cuboid ( , , ), find the angle between the diagonals and .   Triangle across three faces of the cuboid (dashed), and the same triangle drawn flat.   Left: a cuboid ABCDEFGH, AB 12 cm, BC 5 cm, AE 7 cm, with the triangle ACF drawn in thick dashed lines across the base, the front face and the right-hand face. The hidden corner D and its three edges are dashed. Right: triangle ACF drawn flat, with AC 13, AF the square root of 193, CF the square root of 74, and the angle theta at A marked.       Step 1: the triangle. The lines meet at , so the angle is angle in triangle . Each side of this triangle is a diagonal of a face.  Step 2: the sides.   Step 3: a right angle? No: and no other pair of squares adds to the third. Use the cosine rule, with opposite angle : so angle . Keep the squares ( , , ) exactly; rounding the sides first can change the last digit.     Angles at the Top of a Pyramid   A model of a roof is a right pyramid on a square base of side , with height . Find (a) angle , between opposite sloping edges; (b) angle , between neighbouring sloping edges.   The angles and at the top of the pyramid.   A right pyramid VABCD on a square base of side 10 cm with height VO 12 cm drawn dotted. The sloping edges VA, VB and VC are thick; the base diagonal AC is dashed. Angle AVC and angle AVB are marked at V.       (a) Triangle is isosceles, and cuts it into two right-angled triangles. From , . In triangle , with the right angle at : so angle and angle (double before rounding).  (b) Triangle has and . is not in this face, and triangle has no right angle, so use the cosine rule (or split the isosceles triangle at the midpoint of , as in ): so angle .    Choose from each list. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign, for example 36.9 , correct to 1 decimal place.                A cuboid has , and . Find (a) angle ; (b) angle .  (a) (b)  (a) Angle . , so and angle .  (b) Triangle : , , . No right angle, so and angle .    For the tent in ( , , ), find angle , between the bottom edge and the line from a front corner to the back of the ridge.   is at right angles to the back end , which contains , so angle . and , so and angle .    A right pyramid has a rectangular base with and . Each sloping edge is long. Find angles and .  Angle ; angle  Triangle : , so angle . Triangle : , so angle . The longer base edge is opposite the larger angle.    A pyramid has a right angle in its base, , and the edge is vertical. Find angle .   and . By the cosine rule, , so angle .    A grain store at a cereals depot in Eldoret is a cuboid with , and . Wires run from the ceiling corner to the floor corners and . Find the angle between the wires.   is vertical, so triangle has a right angle at . and , so and angle .    Challenge: Two Space Diagonals of a Cube  Show that the angle between two space diagonals of a cube, and , is about , whatever the size of the cube.  The diagonals cross at the centre of the cube. Use the rectangle and the triangle .  Angle , so the angle between the lines is .  Let the edge be . Then and . The diagonals bisect each other at , so . In triangle : The cancels, so the angle does not depend on the size of the cube. Angle is obtuse, so the angle between the two lines is .    "
