  "title": "Transformation of Waves",
  "body": " Transformation of Waves       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.5 Trigonometry III    Specific Learning Outcomes  Transform trigonometric waves using stretch and translation           Stretching Waves   Stretching on Tracing Paper      Trace for . Double the height of every point (for example, mark and ) and draw the new wave. What is its equation?    Now divide every -coordinate of the original wave by 3 (for example, moves to ). What is the equation of the new wave?    Predict the period of , and check by tracing.        Stretching a Wave  In a one-way stretch , every point moves away from the invariant axis by the same factor, the stretch factor .    is the image of under a stretch with the -axis invariant (parallel to the -axis), stretch factor : . The amplitude is multiplied by .  is the image of under a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor : . The period becomes .      Left: (grey) and . Right: (grey) and . The point and its image are marked.   The point (90 degrees, 1) moves to (90 degrees, 3) in the first panel and to (30 degrees, 1) in the second.  Left panel: the grey wave y = sin x and the black wave y = 3 sin x, both crossing the x-axis at 0, 180 and 360 degrees; the black wave reaches 3. Right panel: the grey wave y = sin x and the black wave y = sin 3x, which repeats every 120 degrees and has its first maximum at (30 degrees, 1).      A Stretch Parallel to the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 4. The point maps to . The amplitude becomes 4; the period stays .    A Stretch Parallel to the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor . The point maps to . The period becomes .    Two Stretches  Describe the two stretches that map onto , and find the image of .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2, and a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2 (since ). . The amplitude is 2 and the period .    A Negative Multiplier  Describe how the graph of is obtained from .  A stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2, followed by a reflection in the -axis (the minus sign). The amplitude is 2.   Type decimals with a point, for example 1.73 , or a fraction such as 1\/3 .        Describe the stretch that maps or onto       (a) -axis invariant, stretch factor 5 (b) -axis invariant, stretch factor (c) -axis invariant, stretch factor 3 (d) -axis invariant, stretch factor 0.5    The graph of is stretched with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2. Write down the equation of the image and its period.  ; period .    The graph of is mapped onto . Find the image of the point .     The graph of is mapped onto . Describe both stretches, and find the first maximum point of .  Stretch factor 3 with the -axis invariant; stretch factor with the -axis invariant. First maximum .    A wave with amplitude 2 and period is stretched with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 1.5, then with the -axis invariant, stretch factor . Find the new amplitude and period.  Amplitude 3, period .    Challenge: Does the Order Matter?  Show that the two stretches of give the same graph whichever is done first.  Follow a general point through each order.  Each stretch changes only one coordinate: either order sends to .       Translating Waves and Combining Transformations   Sliding on Tracing Paper      Trace and slide the tracing to the left. Where is the first maximum now? Which equation, or , has its maximum there?    Slide the tracing to the left. What do you notice? Which equation describes the new position?    Trace . How far must you slide it to get ? Test with the maximum.        Translating and Combining    is the image of under the translation : the graph moves the opposite way to the sign.  is the image under the translation .  Since , the graph of is obtained from by, in order: a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor ; a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor ; the translation . Inside the bracket, the graph moves by the angle divided by .  Another order also works for the -direction: first the translation , then the stretch with stretch factor . The size of the translation depends on the order.      (grey) and (black).   The maximum moves from 45 degrees to 10 degrees: a translation of 35 degrees to the left.  The grey wave y = sin 2x has maxima at 45 and 225 degrees. The black wave y = sin(2x + 70 degrees) has the same shape with maxima at 10 and 190 degrees. An arrow from (45 degrees, 1) to (10 degrees, 1) shows the translation of 35 degrees to the left.      The grey wave is . Stretch it, then translate it, and read the equation of the image.     A Translation Along the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto .  The translation , that is to the right. The phase angle is .    A Translation Along the -Axis  Describe the transformation that maps onto .  The translation . The mean line moves from to .    The Shift  Describe how is obtained (a) from (b) from .   (a) , so it is the translation of ( ).  (b) Either a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor , then the translation ; or first the translation (giving ), then the stretch with stretch factor .     A Sequence of Transformations  Describe a sequence of transformations that maps onto , and follow the point through it.   . In order: a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 2; a stretch with the -axis invariant, stretch factor ; the translation .  . Check: and .     From a Description to an Equation  The graph of is stretched with the -axis invariant, stretch factor 3, and then translated by . Write down the equation of the image.  The stretch gives ; the translation subtracts 1: .    Waves Around Us  Sine and cosine waves describe many things that repeat. When a nyatiti string vibrates, the air pressure near it rises and falls like a sine wave: a tighter string gives a shorter period and a higher note, and a harder pluck gives a bigger amplitude and a louder sound. Kenya's mains electricity alternates 50 times a second, so the graph of its voltage against time is a wave with period of a second. The tide at Mombasa rises and falls about twice a day. Radio and phone signals, and the waves from an earthquake or a tsunami, are waves too. Changing the amplitude, the period or the phase of a wave is exactly the stretching and translating of this section.   Type decimals with a point, for example 1.73 , or a fraction such as 1\/3 . Type an equation such as y=3*sin(2*x+60) ; the angle in the bracket is in degrees.                Give the translation vector that maps or onto (a) (b) (c) .  (a) (b) (c)    Give the translation vector that maps onto .     Describe a sequence of transformations that maps onto , and find the image of .  Stretch factor 4 with the -axis invariant; stretch factor with the -axis invariant; translation . Image .  . Check: .    Find the smallest translation to the right, along the -axis, that maps onto . Check your answer at three angles.  : the maximum of at moves to the maximum of at , and ; at , , both give .    The graph of , with , has a maximum point at . Find .     Kamau says that is moved to the right. Is he right?  No. : it is moved to the right.    Challenge: A Reflection That Is a Translation  Show that the graph of is a translation of the graph of .  Compare with at a few angles, then think about the unit circle.  , so the translation maps onto .  On the unit circle the point at is opposite the point at , so its -coordinate is .     "
