  "title": "Ratio Theorem",
  "body": " Ratio Theorem       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.6 Vectors II    Specific Learning Outcomes  Derive and use ratio theorem in vector operations          Deriving the Ratio Theorem   In triangle , and . The point on divides it internally in the ratio .   Triangle with on and .   Triangle OAB. An arrow labelled a goes from O to A and an arrow labelled b from O to B. P is on AB, with AP marked m and PB marked n. An arrow goes from O to P.        What fraction of is ?    Write in terms of and .    Use to write in terms of and . Collect the terms.    Repeat (a) to (c) for a ratio .    Check your formula with , and the ratio . Compare with , part (e).    What do the coefficients of and add to?        The Ratio Theorem  Let and have position vectors and . The point dividing internally in the ratio has position vector The point dividing externally in the ratio has position vector This is the ratio theorem . The weight on is the part of the ratio nearer . For the midpoint, gives , as in Grade 10.  Both forms come from , so the coefficients always add to . Turning this round: a point with position vector , where , lies on the line . It is between and when , beyond when , and beyond when .    Drag and , and move the and sliders. The two arrows from , a multiple of then a multiple of , always end at the point on .     Using the Ratio Theorem   The points and have position vectors and . Find the point that divides internally in the ratio .    With and : so is .     External Division by the Ratio Theorem   is and is . Find the point that divides externally in the ratio .     so is . Check: and , so is three times and is beyond .     Finding the Ratio   For each point, decide whether it divides internally or externally, and find the ratio.          (a) The coefficients add to and is between and , so is between and . The weight on is and the weight on is : internally. (Check: .)  (b) , so is on the line , and puts it beyond . and . The lengths are and , so divides externally in the ratio .     Vectors in use: in 3D animation a point is often placed three fifths of the way between two others; the program computes it with exactly the weights of the ratio theorem.  Type answers in terms of a and b like 3\/5*a+2\/5*b or (3a+2b)\/5 . Type fractions with a slash, for example 3\/4 , not as decimals.                Use the ratio theorem to find the point dividing internally in the ratio , where is and is .     Use the ratio theorem to find the point dividing externally in the ratio , where is and is .   .    In terms of and , write the position vector of the point dividing (a) internally in the ratio ; (b) externally in the ratio .  (a) (b)  (b) . The coefficients and add to , and : the point is beyond .    A water pipe at a school runs straight from a tank to a tap , in metres. A valve divides internally in the ratio . Find the position of .   .    Each point lies on the line . Say whether it divides internally or externally, and give the ratio. (a) (b)  (a) Internally, . (b) Externally, .  (b) and ; is beyond and .    Challenge: The Centroid  In triangle , is the midpoint of . Show that the point dividing internally in the ratio has position vector . Explain why the three medians of the triangle meet at .  The answer is symmetric in , and .  with .  By the ratio theorem, . The same working for the median from (to the midpoint of ) or from gives the same expression, because it does not change when , and are swapped. So the point two thirds of the way along each median is the same point , and all three medians pass through it.    "
