  "number": "10.9",
  "title": "Application of Vectors to Geometry",
  "body": " Application of Vectors to Geometry       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.6 Vectors II    Specific Learning Outcomes  Apply vectors to geometry in different situations          The Midpoint Theorem   Draw any triangle and let and . Mark , the midpoint of , and , the midpoint of .   Triangle with and the midpoints of and .   Triangle OAB with arrow a from O to A and arrow b from O to B. M is the midpoint of OA and N the midpoint of OB. The segment MN is parallel to AB and half as long.        Write and in terms of and .    Find .    Find .    What do (b) and (c) tell you about the lines and ?    Measure and in your own triangle. Does your drawing agree?    Now let and be one third of the way from to and from to . What is ?        Proving with Vectors  To prove a result in geometry with vectors:  Choose two base vectors, such as and , that are not parallel.  Write every vector you need in terms of and , using ratios along lines and end minus start .  To show two lines are parallel, show one vector is a number times the other. To show points are collinear, also show the vectors share a point.     Equating coefficients. If and are not zero and not parallel, and then and . The reason: otherwise would make and parallel. Since and point in different directions, neither can make up for the other.    Drag , and to change the triangle. and stay at the midpoints of and , and the text compares with .     Diagonals of a Parallelogram   is a parallelogram with and . Prove that its diagonals and bisect each other.   Parallelogram with diagonals and .   Parallelogram OABC with OA = a along the bottom and OC = c up the left side. The diagonals OB and AC cross at their common midpoint.        , since . So the midpoint of has position vector .   , so the midpoint of has position vector .  The two midpoints have the same position vector, so they are the same point: the diagonals meet at the midpoint of each, and bisect each other.     A Line Parallel to a Side   In triangle , and . is on with and is on with . Show that is parallel to and find the ratio of their lengths.     and , so  is a positive multiple of , so is parallel to , and .     Where Two Lines Meet   In triangle , and . is on with , and is on with . The lines and meet at . Find the ratios and .   Triangle with the lines and crossing at .   Triangle OAB. C is two thirds of the way from O to A; D is one third of the way from A to B. The lines OD and BC cross at X.        Step 1: write the vectors. By the ratio theorem, . Also , so .   Step 2: two routes to X.  is on , so for some number . is also on , so for some number .   Step 3: equate coefficients.  and are not parallel, so Hence . , so ; and , so .     Vectors Around Us  At the start of this chapter we asked: how do we use vectors to describe positions and movements in space? You can now answer with three numbers for every position and every movement, and the rules for adding, scaling, measuring and dividing them.    A drone spraying a shamba, or an aircraft approaching an airport, has a position and a velocity in three dimensions. Its flight computer adds displacement vectors many times a second.  Structural engineers treat the forces in a roof truss or a mast cable as vectors; a roof stands when the force vectors at each joint add to the zero vector.  Computer games and 3D animation move every point of a character by adding a vector, and make it larger or smaller with a scalar multiple.  A GPS receiver works out its position in three dimensions from the distances to several satellites, using the magnitude formula of .  Navigation by bearings and distances, in , uses the same ideas in the plane.    Which of these uses could you see in your own county? Discuss with a partner, and explain one of them to the class in your own words.   Type answers in terms of a and b like 3\/5*a+2\/5*b or (3a+2b)\/5 . Type fractions with a slash, for example 3\/4 , not as decimals.            is a parallelogram with and , and is the midpoint of . Write , and in terms of and .  ; ;    In triangle , , and is the midpoint of . divides internally in the ratio . Find and .  ;     is a trapezium with , and . is on with .    Find .  Find .  What do your answers tell you about the lines and ?     (a) (b) (c) and are parallel and the same length.  (a) . (b) , so . (c) : equal vectors, so is parallel to and the same length. (So is a parallelogram.)    A farmer near Kitale has a triangular plot. Paths join the midpoints of the three sides. Show that each path is parallel to one side of the plot and half as long.  The midpoint theorem, applied three times.  Call the corners , , , with and , and let , , be the midpoints of , , . As in , . Also , so and . Each path is half of the side it does not touch, and parallel to it.    In triangle , and . is the midpoint of and is on with . The lines and meet at . Find and .  ;  , so . , so . Equating coefficients: and , so , and . So and .    Challenge: Midpoints of a Quadrilateral  Prove that the midpoints of the four sides of any quadrilateral are the corners of a parallelogram.  Compare each side of the inner shape with a diagonal of .  Each side of the inner shape is half of a diagonal: two sides are and two are .  Let the midpoints of , , , be , , , , with position vectors and so on. Then and . So : one pair of opposite sides is equal and parallel, and is a parallelogram. (In the same way .)    "
