  "title": "Distance and Speed",
  "body": " Distance and Speed       Strand  2.0 Measurements and Geometry    Sub-Strand  2.7 Navigation    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine speed in knots and in kilometres per hour          The Ship to Dar es Salaam   A passenger ship sails due south from Mombasa to Dar es Salaam. Take both ports to be on the meridian , with Mombasa at latitude and Dar es Salaam at latitude . The ship leaves Mombasa at 18:00 on Monday and arrives in Dar es Salaam at 07:45 on Tuesday.     How far does the ship sail, in nautical miles?    How long does the voyage take? Give your answer in hours as a decimal.    Find the ship's average speed in nautical miles per hour.    Sailors call a speed of nautical mile per hour a knot . One nautical mile is about . Find the ship's speed in kilometres per hour.        Speed in Knots  A knot is a speed of one nautical mile per hour. Ships and aircraft give their speeds in knots because their charts measure distance in nautical miles.   with the distance in nautical miles, the time in hours and the speed in knots.  Since , a speed of knot is . Write a time in hours as a decimal before dividing: .    A Flight Along the Equator   An aircraft flies due west along the equator from longitude to longitude . The flight takes . Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.    Both longitudes are east, so the difference in longitude is . The equator is a great circle, so the distance is . The time is .     Sailing East Along a Parallel   A cargo ship leaves Mombasa, taken to be at , and sails due east along the parallel at for hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.    Step 1: distance.  .  Step 2: change in longitude. Along the parallel , one degree of longitude is long. So to the nearest minute ( ).  Step 3: new longitude. Sailing east increases an east longitude: . The new position is .     From Kilometres per Hour to Knots   An airliner cruises at . Find its speed in knots, and the time it takes to fly , to the nearest minute.    . Time . The decimal part is minutes, so the flight takes .    Type decimals with a point, for example 36.8 . Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.  Give times on the 24-hour clock: type the hours and the minutes in separate boxes, for example 14 and 05 for 14:05.                A ship sails along the meridian from latitude to latitude in days hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour.  ;  Opposite sides of the equator, so add: , so . Time . Speed  .    Convert to kilometres per hour.   One knot is an hour, and . So , which is .    A plane flies along the parallel from longitude to longitude at . How long does the flight take?  hours  Difference in longitude . Distance . Time .    A ship sails along the parallel from longitude to longitude in hours. Find its speed in knots and in kilometres per hour, to 1 decimal place.  ;  Difference in longitude . Distance . Speed ; .    Challenge: A New Position  A ship at sails due west along its parallel at for hours. Find its new position, to the nearest minute.  Find the distance first, then divide by the length of one degree of longitude on this parallel.   Distance . Change in longitude . Sailing west decreases an east longitude: .    "
