  "title": "Sketching a Curve.",
  "body": " Sketching a Curve  The stationary points and the intercepts are the skeleton of the curve. A sketch shows:  the -intercept (put );  the -intercepts that can be found exactly, from factorised form, a given factor or the factor theorem; if one cannot be found exactly, say between which whole numbers it lies;  every stationary point, with its coordinates and nature;  the behaviour for large positive and large negative ;  labelled axes and a smooth curve.    For large the term with the highest power is the largest. A cubic with rises to the right and falls to the left; with it falls to the right. A quartic with rises at both ends; with it falls at both ends.  "
