  "id": "sec-area-under-a-curve-by-integration",
  "level": "1",
  "url": "sec-area-under-a-curve-by-integration.html",
  "type": "Section",
  "number": "14.3",
  "title": "Area Under a Curve by Integration",
  "body": " Area Under a Curve by Integration       Strand  4.0 Calculus    Sub-Strand  4.2 Integration    Specific Learning Outcomes  Relate integration to the area under a curve in different situations; Determine area under a curve by integration           Area and the Definite Integral   Area Under a Straight Line   A plan of a shamba drawn on squared paper shows a region bounded by the line , the -axis, the -axis and the line . Let be its area.   The area under from to , and .   Three small graphs of the line y = 2x + 1, each with the region under it shaded from x = 0 to x = 1, 2 and 3. Each region is a trapezium.        Each region is a trapezium. Find , and .    Guess a formula for .    Differentiate . What do you notice?    Find the area under the line from to as . Check it with the area of a trapezium.    Evaluate and compare.        Area Under a Curve  If for , the area of the region between the curve , the -axis and the lines and is , where is any integral of . The region is shaded in . Areas are in square units, or in when the lengths are in metres.    Why Integration Gives Area  Let be the area under the curve from up to . Moving on by a small amount adds a thin strip of width and height about , so . Dividing by and letting the strip get thinner gives : the area function is an integral of .    Drag and along the -axis. When part of the region is below the axis, the integral and the area differ.     Area Under a Parabola  Find the area of the region between the curve , the -axis and the lines and .    The region under from to .   The curve y = x squared + 2 for x from 0 to 3.5, with the region between the curve and the x-axis from x = 1 to x = 3 shaded. Its area is 38\/3 square units.     The curve is above the -axis, so square units.     Area Under a Cubic  Find the area between the curve , the -axis and the lines and .   For , , so the curve is above the axis. The area is square units.     A Shamba With a Curved Boundary  A shamba in Nyeri is drawn on a plan with axes in units of . It is bounded by the -axis (a road), the lines and (two fences) and a stream along the curve . Find the area of the shamba in square metres.   Check the curve is above the axis: at , at and at , and a parabola that bends down stays above its two end heights between them. So square units. One square unit is , so the shamba is about .    Type exact values as whole numbers, fractions or decimals, for example 27\/4 or 6.75 .        Find the area under from to .  square units    Find the area under from to .  square units    Find the area under from to .  square units    Find the area between and the axes, from to , by integration. Check your answer with the area of a triangle.  square units;  . The region is a triangle with base and height .    The opening of a culvert under a farm road in Kiambu is the region between the curve and the -axis (the stream bed), for , in metres. Find the area of the opening.   .    The area under from to , where , is . Find .   , and , so .    Challenge: Equal Areas  The area under from to equals the area under the same curve from to , where is a positive constant. Find .  Write both areas in terms of .   and . So and .       Areas Above and Below the -axis   A Ditch and a Mound   A farmer in Nakuru digs a drainage ditch and piles the soil beside it. Across the ditch and the mound, the ground profile is metres for , where is the original ground level.   The ground profile : a ditch below ground level and a mound above it.   Ground profile curve y = (x squared minus 4x)\/4 for x from 0 to 6 metres. From x = 0 to 4 it dips below ground level to a depth of 1 m at x = 2 (the ditch, hatched); from x = 4 to 6 it rises to 3 m at x = 6 (the mound, shaded). Each cross-section is 8\/3 square metres.        For which values of is the ground below the original level? How deep is the ditch at its deepest?    Evaluate .    What is the area of the cross-section of the ditch?    Evaluate . How high is the mound?    Evaluate . What does it tell you? What is the total area between the profile and ground level?        Regions Below the Axis  The definite integral adds up thin strips of height . Below the -axis is negative, so a region below the axis gives a negative integral; its area is the size of the integral.  When the curve crosses the axis between the limits, find the -intercepts, integrate each part separately, and add the sizes of the answers. Always sketch the curve first.    Integrating Across the Axis  One integral across a crossing point gives the area above the axis minus the area below it, not their sum. In it gives .    Area Enclosed by a Parabola  Find the area enclosed by the curve and the -axis.   The curve crosses the axis at and , and is above it between them (at , ). Expand: . The area is square units.     A Region Below the Axis  Find the area between the curve and the -axis.   , so the curve crosses the axis at and and is below it between them. The integral is negative because the region is below the axis. The area is square units.     A Region Above and Below  Find the total area between the curve , the -axis and the lines and .    The curve for : a region below the axis from to and a region above it from to .   The curve y = x cubed minus 4x from x = 0 to 3. It dips below the x-axis between 0 and 2 (hatched region, area 4) and rises above it between 2 and 3 (shaded region, area 25\/4).     , so between and the curve crosses the axis at . Integrate each part, with : The total area is square units. The single integral is not the area: the part below the axis has cancelled part of the area above.    Type exact values as whole numbers, fractions or decimals, for example 27\/4 or 6.75 . Type a polynomial with ^ for powers, for example 3-2x .                    Find the area enclosed by the curve and the -axis.  square units  The curve crosses the axis at . .    Find the area between the curve and the -axis.  square units  , below the axis for . , so the area is .    Find the total area enclosed by the curve and the -axis.  square units  , crossing at , , . With : (above) and (below). Total area .    A pipe joins two tanks. Water flows through it at litres per second for , into the first tank when is positive and out of it when is negative. Find the net change in the amount of water in the first tank, and the total amount of water that passes through the pipe.  Net change ; total  at . in; , so out. Net change (which is ); total through the pipe .    Evaluate . Find the area between , the -axis and the lines and .  ; square units  . Split at : each part has size , so the area is .    Challenge: An Unknown Root  The curve , where , encloses an area of square units with the -axis. Find .  Show that the area is .   The curve crosses the axis at and . . So and .     "
