  "title": "Errors in Area Approximation Under a Curve",
  "body": " Errors in Area Approximation Under a Curve       Strand  4.0 Calculus    Sub-Strand  4.2 Integration    Specific Learning Outcomes  Determine errors from using integration and area approximation methods          Akinyi and Baraka Compare   Akinyi and Baraka, Grade 12 learners in Kisumu, want to know how good their estimates of the area under from to are. Akinyi uses the trapezoidal rule and Baraka the mid-ordinate rule.     Find the exact area by integration.    Find Akinyi's and Baraka's estimates with strips and with strips. Read the values for strips from the interactive in .    For each estimate work out estimate exact value.    Express the size of each of these as a percentage of the exact value.    What happens to the errors each time the number of strips doubles?    Compare Akinyi's error with Baraka's for the same number of strips.        Errors in Area Approximation  When an estimate is compared with the exact value found by integration: A positive error means the estimate is too big. The absolute error is the size of the error, and the percentage error compares it with the exact value.  These are the absolute and percentage errors of Grade 11 (Approximations and Errors), with the exact value from integration in place of the working value; the relative error, absolute error divided by exact value, is the percentage error before multiplying by . The signed error is new: its sign shows whether the estimate is too big or too small.  For every polynomial of degree at most , two patterns hold exactly:  halve the strip width (double the number of strips) and the error of each rule falls to a quarter;  the mid-ordinate error is half the trapezoidal error, with the opposite sign.      Other Curves  For other curves the two patterns are not exact, but they hold very nearly once the strips are narrow.    Using a Spreadsheet  A spreadsheet does the arithmetic for many strips. Put the values of in column A (start at and add each row), the heights in column B by a formula such as =A2^3 , and the weights in column C, and in cell E1. With rows 2 to 10 ( ), =SUMPRODUCT(B2:B10,C2:C10)*E1\/2 gives the trapezoidal estimate. Change and the number of rows to change , and watch the error fall.    Watch how the percentage errors fall as grows: doubling divides them by .     Integration in real life: when land is sold by the square metre, a small percentage error in an estimated area can cost the buyer or the seller thousands of shillings, as the last example shows.   Percentage Error of an Estimate   In the trapezoidal estimate of the area under from to was , the mid-ordinate estimate , and the exact area . Find the absolute error and the percentage error of each estimate.    Trapezoidal: absolute error , and Mid-ordinate: absolute error , and the percentage error is .     Predicting the Error   The trapezoidal estimate of with strips is . Predict and without calculating them.    The exact value is , so the error of is . Doubling the strips divides the error by : the errors of and are and , and they stay positive. So and , the values found in .     The Price of an Error   A plot in Nyeri lies between a straight fence and a river. Measured from one end of the fence, the width of the plot metres along it is metres, for , as in . Land here sells at Ksh per .   The plot by the river in Nyeri, with its width measured every .   A plot 40 m long between a straight fence along the bottom and a curved river bank along the top. Widths measured every 10 m are 30, 34, 36, 36 and 34 m at 0, 10, 20, 30 and 40 m along the fence.  Horizontal axis: distance along the fence, x, from 0 to 40 m. Vertical axis: width y in metres from 0 to 40. The river bank is the curve y = 30 + 0.5x - 0.01x squared. Vertical dashed lines at x = 0, 10, 20, 30, 40 are labelled with the widths 30, 34, 36, 36, 34 m.       Find the exact area of the plot.  Find the trapezoidal and mid-ordinate estimates with strips wide, and the percentage error of each, correct to 2 decimal places.  If the plot were priced from the trapezoidal estimate, by how much would its value be wrong?      (a)  (b) With the widths are , so . The mid-widths at are , so .  The absolute error of is , a percentage error of . For it is , about . The boundary bends downwards, so is too small and too big.  (c) : priced from the trapezoidal estimate, the plot would be undervalued by about Ksh .    Type exact values as whole numbers, fractions or decimals, for example 27\/4 or 6.75 .                For from to , find the trapezoidal estimate with strips, the exact area, the absolute error and the percentage error.  ; exact ; absolute error ;  Ordinates : . Exact . Absolute error ; .    Repeat with the mid-ordinate rule.  ; absolute error ;  Mid-ordinates : . Absolute error ; .    For from to , find the exact area, the trapezoidal estimate with strips and its percentage error. Then predict without calculating it.  Exact ; ; ;  Exact . Ordinates : , error , percentage error . The error of is , so .    Otieno found that an area is exactly , and his trapezoidal estimate was . He wrote: percentage error . What is wrong? Give the correct percentage error.  He divided by the estimate; divide by the exact value: .    A drainage channel on a tea estate in Kericho is wide. Its depth metres from one side is metres. Using strips wide, find the mid-ordinate and trapezoidal estimates of the cross-section area, the exact area, and the percentage error of each estimate.  , , exact ; and  Mid-depths at : , total , so . Depths at : , so . Exact: . Percentage errors: for and for .    Challenge: Combining the Rules (Extension)  For from to with strips, and . Show that is exactly the area. Explain why this works for every polynomial of degree at most .  Use the second error pattern: the mid-ordinate error is of the trapezoidal error.  , the exact area.  . If the exact area is and , then , so and the errors cancel. This combination is called Simpson's rule; it is beyond the KICD Grade 12 design.    "
